 "footnotes": [
  {
   "id": "footnote-1",
   "kind": "book",
   "number": "1",
   "html": "This relationship between bandwidth and <span data-concept=\"correlation-time\">correlation time</span> of the <span data-concept=\"open-system\">reservoir</span> is often, but not always, true. For example, if the system consists of an ensemble of atoms with spatial extent $L$ larger than the optical wavelength $\\lambda_0$, and the <span data-concept=\"open-system\">environment</span> consists of the electromagnetic field, the <span data-concept=\"correlation-time\">correlation time</span> $\\ktauc \\sim L/c$ corresponds to the time it takes for a photon to leave the medium, since a photon released by one atom can be absorbed by another a time $\\ktauc$ later."
  },
  {
   "id": "footnote-2",
   "kind": "book",
   "number": "2",
   "html": "The <span data-concept=\"born-markov-approximation\">Born approximation</span> is actually slightly stronger than this. It states that the <span data-concept=\"open-system\">reservoir</span> is large enough that the assumption of factorizability is good at all times as far as calculating the system dynamics is concerned."
  },
  {
   "id": "c-eq-3-33",
   "kind": "correction",
   "html": "The book prints $+i$ in the exponent. The evolution operator of $\\kHSR = \\hbar\\kft(\\kt)\\hat{S}_z$ is $\\hat{U} = e^{-i\\int_0^{\\kt}\\kft(\\kt')d\\kt'\\hat{S}_z}$, since $\\kHSR$ at different times commutes with itself; with it $\\hat{U}^\\dagger\\hat{S}_+\\hat{U} = e^{i\\int_0^{\\kt}\\kft(\\kt')d\\kt'}\\hat{S}_+$, the phase of Eq. (3.34)."
  }
 ],
 "derivations": [
  {
   "id": "density-operator-evolution",
   "what": "the equation of motion of the density operator",
   "source": "Modern Atomic and Optical Physics II, 3.3 System-reservoir interactions",
   "nodes": [
    {
     "assumes": [
      {
       "text": "The probabilities $p_\\alpha$ of the mixture are constant in time.",
       "src": "(3.1)"
      },
      {
       "tex": "i\\hbar|\\dot{\\kpsi}_\\alpha^S\\rangle = \\kHS|\\kpsi_\\alpha^S\\rangle",
       "src": "(1.11)"
      },
      {
       "text": "The conjugate of the Schroedinger equation, $-i\\hbar\\langle\\dot{\\kpsi}_\\alpha^S| = \\langle\\kpsi_\\alpha^S|\\kHS$, since $\\kHS$ is Hermitian.",
       "src": "(1.11), (1.5)"
      }
     ],
     "then": {
      "tex": "\\mk{L}{\\krhoS} = \\mk{R}{\\sum_\\alpha p_\\alpha|\\kpsi_\\alpha^S\\rangle\\langle\\kpsi_\\alpha^S|}",
      "src": "(3.1)",
      "uses": []
     },
     "steps": [
      {
       "tex": "\\mk{L}{i\\hbar\\dot{\\krhoS}} = \\mk{R}{\\sum_\\alpha p_\\alpha(i\\hbar|\\dot{\\kpsi}_\\alpha^S\\rangle\\langle\\kpsi_\\alpha^S| + |\\kpsi_\\alpha^S\\rangle\\,i\\hbar\\langle\\dot{\\kpsi}_\\alpha^S|)}",
       "uses": [
        0
       ],
       "cap": "Differentiating by the product rule and multiplying by $i\\hbar$."
      },
      {
       "tex": "\\mk{L}{i\\hbar\\dot{\\krhoS}} = \\mk{R}{\\sum_\\alpha p_\\alpha(\\kHS|\\kpsi_\\alpha^S\\rangle\\langle\\kpsi_\\alpha^S| - |\\kpsi_\\alpha^S\\rangle\\langle\\kpsi_\\alpha^S|\\kHS)}",
       "uses": [
        1,
        2
       ]
      },
      {
       "tex": "\\mk{L}{i\\hbar\\dot{\\krhoS}} = \\mk{R}{\\kHS\\krhoS - \\krhoS\\kHS}",
       "uses": []
      },
      {
       "tex": "\\mk{L}{\\dot{\\krhoS}} = \\mk{R}{\\frac{1}{i\\hbar}[\\kHS, \\krhoS]}",
       "uses": []
      }
     ],
     "num": "(3.23)",
     "note": "The book prints $\\frac{1}{i\\hbar}[\\kHS, \\krhoS]$ as equal to $i\\hbar\\dot{\\krhoS}$ of Eq. (3.22); the factor $\\frac{1}{i\\hbar}$ belongs with $\\dot{\\krhoS}$, the form of Eq. (3.28)."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "decaying-atom-density-matrix",
   "what": "the reduced density matrix of a decaying atom",
   "source": "Modern Atomic and Optical Physics II, 3.3 System-reservoir interactions",
   "nodes": [
    {
     "assumes": [
      {
       "tex": "|\\kpsi(\\kt)\\rangle = \\kcone|1\\rangle \\otimes |0\\rangle + \\kctwo(\\kt)|2\\rangle \\otimes |0\\rangle + \\sum_k \\kck(\\kt)|1\\rangle \\otimes |1_k\\rangle",
       "src": "3.3"
      },
      {
       "text": "In the frame rotating at the atomic frequency $\\komega$, the excited amplitude decays as $\\kctwo(\\kt) = \\kctwo e^{-\\kgamma\\kt/2}$.",
       "src": "(2.90)"
      },
      {
       "text": "Probability is conserved: $|\\kcone|^2 + |\\kctwo(\\kt)|^2 + \\sum_k|\\kck(\\kt)|^2 = 1$.",
       "src": "2.3.4"
      },
      {
       "text": "For the initial pure state the density matrix has $\\rho_{ij}(0) = c_i c_j^*$.",
       "src": "3.1"
      }
     ],
     "then": {
      "tex": "\\mk{L}{\\krhoS} = \\mk{R}{\\mathrm{Tr}_R[|\\kpsi(\\kt)\\rangle\\langle\\kpsi(\\kt)|]}",
      "src": "(3.21)",
      "uses": []
     },
     "steps": [
      {
       "tex": "\\mk{L}{\\krhoS} = \\mk{R}{\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty\\sum_k\\langle n_k|\\kpsi(\\kt)\\rangle\\langle\\kpsi(\\kt)|n_k\\rangle}",
       "uses": [],
       "cap": "The trace over the field runs over the $n$-photon Fock states $|n_k\\rangle$ of every mode $k$."
      },
      {
       "tex": "\\mk{L}{\\krhoS} = \\mk{R}{\\Big(|\\kcone|^2 + \\sum_k|\\kck(\\kt)|^2\\Big)|1\\rangle\\langle 1| + |\\kctwo(\\kt)|^2|2\\rangle\\langle 2| + \\kcone\\kctwo(\\kt)^*|1\\rangle\\langle 2| + \\kctwo(\\kt)\\kcone^*|2\\rangle\\langle 1|}",
       "uses": [
        0
       ],
       "cap": "Only $\\langle 0|$ meets the parts with no photon, and only $\\langle 1_k|$ meets $c_k(t)|1\\rangle \\otimes |1_k\\rangle$."
      },
      {
       "tex": "\\mk{L}{\\krhoS} = \\mk{R}{\\begin{pmatrix} |\\kcone|^2 + \\sum_k|\\kck(\\kt)|^2 & \\kcone\\kctwo^* e^{-\\kgamma {\\kt}/2} \\\\ \\kctwo\\kcone^* e^{-\\kgamma {\\kt}/2} & |\\kctwo|^2 e^{-\\kgamma {\\kt}} \\end{pmatrix}}",
       "uses": [
        1
       ],
       "cap": "As a matrix in the basis $\\{|1\\rangle, |2\\rangle\\}$."
      },
      {
       "tex": "\\mk{L}{\\krhoS} = \\mk{R}{\\begin{pmatrix} 1 - \\krhotwotwo(0)e^{-\\kgamma {\\kt}} & \\krhoonetwo(0)e^{-\\kgamma {\\kt}/2} \\\\ \\krhotwoone(0)e^{-\\kgamma {\\kt}/2} & \\krhotwotwo(0)e^{-\\kgamma {\\kt}} \\end{pmatrix}}",
       "uses": [
        2,
        3
       ]
      }
     ],
     "num": "(3.26)"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "reduced-density-equation",
   "what": "the equation of motion of the reduced density operator",
   "source": "Modern Atomic and Optical Physics II, 3.3.1 General formalism",
   "nodes": [
    {
     "assumes": [
      {
       "tex": "\\kH = \\kHS + \\kHR + \\kHSR",
       "src": "(3.27)"
      },
      {
       "tex": "\\krhoS = \\mathrm{Tr}_R[\\krhohat]",
       "src": "(3.21)"
      },
      {
       "text": "$\\kHS$ acts only on the system degrees of freedom, so the trace over the reservoir passes it.",
       "src": "3.3.1"
      },
      {
       "text": "The trace is invariant under cyclic permutations of the reservoir operators, so $\\mathrm{Tr}_R[[\\kHR, \\krhohat]] = 0$.",
       "src": "3.3.1"
      }
     ],
     "then": {
      "tex": "\\mk{L}{\\dot{\\krhohat}} = \\mk{R}{\\frac{1}{i\\hbar}[\\kH, \\krhohat]}",
      "src": "(3.28)",
      "uses": []
     },
     "steps": [
      {
       "tex": "\\mk{L}{\\dot{\\krhohat}} = \\mk{R}{\\frac{1}{i\\hbar}[\\kHS + \\kHR + \\kHSR, \\krhohat]}",
       "uses": [
        0
       ]
      },
      {
       "tex": "\\mk{L}{\\dot{\\krhoS}} = \\mk{R}{\\frac{1}{i\\hbar}\\mathrm{Tr}_R[[\\kHS + \\kHR + \\kHSR, \\krhohat]]}",
       "uses": [
        1
       ],
       "cap": "Tracing over the reservoir degrees of freedom on both sides."
      },
      {
       "tex": "\\mk{L}{\\dot{\\krhoS}} = \\mk{s}{\\frac{1}{i\\hbar}\\mathrm{Tr}_R[[\\kHS, \\krhohat]]} + \\mk{r}{\\frac{1}{i\\hbar}\\mathrm{Tr}_R[[\\kHR, \\krhohat]]} + \\mk{i}{\\frac{1}{i\\hbar}\\mathrm{Tr}_R[[\\kHSR, \\krhohat]]}",
       "uses": [],
       "map": {
        "R": [
         "s",
         "r",
         "i"
        ]
       }
      },
      {
       "tex": "\\mk{L}{\\dot{\\krhoS}} = \\mk{s}{\\frac{1}{i\\hbar}[\\kHS, \\krhoS]} + \\mk{r}{\\frac{1}{i\\hbar}\\mathrm{Tr}_R[[\\kHR, \\krhohat]]} + \\mk{i}{\\frac{1}{i\\hbar}\\mathrm{Tr}_R[[\\kHSR, \\krhohat]]}",
       "uses": [
        1,
        2
       ]
      },
      {
       "tex": "\\mk{L}{\\dot{\\krhoS}} = \\mk{s}{\\frac{1}{i\\hbar}[\\kHS, \\krhoS]} + \\mk{r}{\\frac{1}{i\\hbar}\\underbrace{\\mathrm{Tr}_R[[\\kHR, \\krhohat]]}_{=0}} + \\mk{i}{\\frac{1}{i\\hbar}\\mathrm{Tr}_R[[\\kHSR, \\krhohat]]}",
       "uses": [
        3
