   "id": "first-reservoir-term",
   "what": "the first-order effect of the reservoir",
   "source": "Modern Atomic and Optical Physics II, 3.3.3 Density operator method for a Markovian reservoir",
   "nodes": [
    {
     "assumes": [
      {
       "tex": "\\kHtilde_{SR} = \\kShat\\kFR({\\kt})^\\dagger + \\kFR({\\kt})\\kShat^\\dagger",
       "src": "(3.42)"
      },
      {
       "text": "The state is factorizable, $\\krhotilde({\\kt}) = \\krhotilde_S({\\kt}) \\otimes \\krhotilde_R({\\kt})$, and the correction $\\krhotilde_{corr}$ is neglected to leading order, since $\\kDeltat \\ll \\ktauS$.",
       "src": "(3.49), (3.50)"
      },
      {
       "tex": "\\langle\\kFR({\\kt}')^\\dagger\\rangle = \\mathrm{Tr}_R\\{\\kFR({\\kt}')^\\dagger\\krhotilde_R({\\kt})\\}",
       "src": "(3.53)"
      }
     ],
     "then": {
      "tex": "\\mk{L}{\\Delta\\krhotilde_S^{(1)}} = \\mk{R}{\\frac{1}{i\\hbar}\\int_{\\kt}^{{\\kt}+\\kDeltat}d{\\kt}'\\ \\mathrm{Tr}_R[\\kHtilde_{SR}({\\kt}'), \\krhotilde({\\kt})]}",
      "src": "(3.48)",
      "uses": []
     },
     "steps": [
      {
       "tex": "\\mk{L}{\\Delta\\krhotilde_S^{(1)}} = \\mk{R}{\\frac{1}{i\\hbar}\\int_{\\kt}^{{\\kt}+\\kDeltat}d{\\kt}'\\ \\mathrm{Tr}_R[\\kShat\\kFR({\\kt}')^\\dagger + \\kFR({\\kt}')\\kShat^\\dagger, \\krhotilde_S({\\kt}) \\otimes \\krhotilde_R({\\kt})]}",
       "uses": [
        0,
        1
       ]
      },
      {
       "tex": "\\mk{L}{\\Delta\\krhotilde_S^{(1)}} = \\mk{R}{\\frac{1}{i\\hbar}\\int_{\\kt}^{{\\kt}+\\kDeltat}d{\\kt}'\\ [\\kShat, \\krhotilde_S({\\kt})] \\otimes \\mathrm{Tr}_R\\{\\kFR({\\kt}')^\\dagger\\krhotilde_R({\\kt})\\} - h.c.}",
       "uses": [],
       "cap": "The system operators come out of the trace over the reservoir, which is cyclic in the reservoir operators, so the terms in $\\hat{S}$ give $[\\hat{S}, \\tilde{\\rho}_S]\\,\\mathrm{Tr}_R\\{\\hat{F}_R^\\dagger\\tilde{\\rho}_R\\}$; the terms in $\\hat{S}^\\dagger$ are minus the Hermitian conjugate of these, since $\\tilde{\\rho}_S$ and $\\tilde{\\rho}_R$ are Hermitian."
